From d89a73ff60852da7607d060179bc0b69e081d1ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NayiemW <37852059+NayiemW@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 04:44:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop the node when its state cannot be built or is
 inconsistent

StateBuilder.run() no longer swallows its errors. When the state
cannot be built or is inconsistent, it terminates the application with
the reason "State Generation failed: <message>" (the error's stack is
logged with it) and then exits the process with code 1. A wallet with
a negative balance, the genesis sender included, gives "State
Generation failed: Wallet with negative balance" (the old text said
"Non-genesis", which patch 40 made wrong). A negative delegate vote
balance gives "... Wallet with negative vote balance", as before.

In s1-ref-v1 run() caught every exception and only logged it, so the
rule that no wallet may hold a negative balance (patch 40) was not
enforced at node start: the node went on to STARTED on the bad state.

Why exit as well: Application.terminate() only disposes the service
providers. On a relay node it left the process running with its pool
workers but no API and no P2P port, so a supervisor never saw a
failure. The exit follows Solar's own handling of a corrupted state
(blockchain.ts and process-blocks-job.ts call process.exit(1)). The
terminate is awaited first, so the providers are disposed and the log
is flushed before the exit. The exit is in a finally around the
terminate, so a terminate that throws (a service provider whose dispose
fails) still exits.

Checked on a running devnet node whose genesis configuration names
the generator key in upper case, so that the transfer bootstrap of
s1-ref-v1 debits the generator (patch 49 fixes that cause): the log
shows "Wallet dZwTMWLN... has a negative balance of
-10000000000000000", "State Generation failed: Wallet with negative
balance" and the stack, and the relay process is gone within a second.
With terminate alone it was still running two minutes later.

Only node start changes; the block rules do not.

Test: patches/tests/45-state-builder-fail-closed.test.js (stub wallets,
process.exit stubbed). For a negative genesis sender, a -1 balance and
a negative vote balance, run() terminates with the reason and exits 1
(base: resolves); when terminate() itself throws, the process still
exits 1; on the Initialise path nothing runs after the StateBuilder
(base: STARTED, then restoreCurrentRound, readdTransactions,
networkMonitor.boot). A consistent state still resolves and starts.

Patch: 45
Consensus: no
